We need to decide whether reference [3] (WHIRL paper) supports what FastUMI uses it for. FastUMI cites [3] in three sentences:

1. "The scarcity of large-scale, high-quality, real-world interaction data remains a major bottleneck to progress in robotic manipulation, primarily due to challenges associated with efficient and scalable data collection methods [3, 10, 50]."

2. "Current methods can be broadly categorized into teleoperation-based techniques [51, 16, 47, 42], vision-driven demonstrations [3, 26], and sensor-enhanced interfaces [6, 40, 48]."

3. "Depending on the nature of the collected data, IL algorithms can leverage real-robot demonstrations [49, 19, 35], video-based observations without explicit action labels [40, 3], or data obtained from decoupled handheld tracking devices [10, 14, 33]."

So FastUMI uses [3] to support claims about: (a) scarcity of large-scale real-world interaction data being a bottleneck; (b) vision-driven demonstrations as a category of data collection methods; (c) video-based observations without explicit action labels as a type of data for IL.

We need to see if WHIRL paper supports these. The WHIRL paper is about learning from human videos (third-person) without action labels, one-shot imitation in the wild. It discusses that traditional IL relies on kinesthetic/teleoperated demonstrations which are expensive, and that visual imitation from human videos is a scalable alternative. It also mentions that data can be expensive to obtain in real world. Let's find relevant quotes.

From the paper: "A popular alternative is to use imitation learning (IL) based approaches, but common IL approaches rely on lots of kinesthetic or teleoperated demonstrations per task. However, this data can be expensive to obtain in the real world and may not be generalizable to new settings or different robots." That supports the scarcity/bottleneck claim.

Also: "To move towards general robot manipulation out of the lab and tabletop settings, we believe visually imitating humans provide a safe and scalable alternative. Rather than asking humans to teleoperate, robot should observe humans to learn as they interact in the world." That supports vision-driven demonstrations.

Also: "In the visual imitation framework, the agent observes other agents perform action without access to actions (just the pixels)." That supports video-based observations without explicit action labels.

So the cited paper clearly backs the claims. The claim in one sentence: FastUMI relies on [3] to support that large-scale real-world interaction data is scarce due to collection challenges, and that vision-driven demonstrations / video-based observations without action labels are a viable data source for IL.

